#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Lazarus Deep: a tile-based survival horror game of a dead mining station
under the ice, in Python + Tk.

Standard library only.  The engine is the one Miskatonic, Hyperspace
Homeroom, Clover Vale and Penultimate share (one survivor, a map of squares,
people to talk to by keyword, a store, places underneath), and the rules are
those of the Mothership sci-fi horror roleplaying game: four Stats and three
Saves rolled on percentile dice, Skills that add to them, Stress that piles
up with every failure until you Panic, Wounds, and a universe that does not
care whether you live.

Lazarus Deep, a Halvorsen-Kade ice mine on Tisiphone, a frozen moon of the
gas giant Megaera.  Nineteen days ago the station stopped shipping ice and
stopped answering.  You are the first of the four contract crew of the tug
Patience to come out of cryo, and the station has just clamped your ship to
its docking ring and will not let it go.  Somewhere under the ice, in the
dark at the bottom of the Dig, something is singing.  The Company's ship Due
Diligence arrives on the evening of the fourth day.  It is not coming to
help.

What happened here, who is still what they seem, who can be saved, and how
to make it stop, are for you to find out.  You may not manage all of it.
Nobody ever does.

Every new game makes a new station: its survivors change, one of them is not
a survivor at all, and the things you need lie in different places.  The
game is saved to ~/.lazarus.json whenever you change place and when you
quit, and resumed next time.  If you die, the next of the Patience's crew
comes out of cryo and carries on.

Crew
  Roll 2d10+25 for each Stat (Strength, Speed, Intellect, Combat) and 2d10+10
  for each Save (Sanity, Fear, Body), then pick a class (Marine, Android,
  Scientist or Teamster) and a speciality.  To do something risky, roll d100
  under the Stat plus a Skill (+10 Trained, +15 Expert, +20 Master); 90-99
  always fails, and doubles are critical.  Advantage [+] rolls twice and takes
  the better, Disadvantage [-] the worse.

Stress and Panic
  Every failed roll is a point of Stress.  A critical failure, a horror or a
  death calls for a Panic Check: a d20 that must beat your Stress, or you
  roll on the Panic table.  Rest in a bunk to shed Stress (a Rest Save).

Wounds
  Damage comes off Health (armour ignores hits below its Armor Points); at
  zero you take a Wound, roll on the Wounds table, and Health resets.  Run
  out of Wounds and you make a Death Save.  Bleeding kills you a point at a
  time: stop it.

Talking
  Walk into someone, or into a counter with someone behind it, then choose or
  type a word.  Everyone knows NAME, JOB and BYE; most know NEWS, and words
  in CAPITALS are worth asking about.  The quartermaster answers BUY.  Help
  someone and they'll trust you: ask the people you trust to go aboard the
  PATIENCE, or to COME with you.

Touch and mouse
  Tap a square to walk there, tap next to you to step, and tap the menus.
  Gamepad (Linux, the Steam Deck): the d-pad or stick moves, A waits or
  chooses, B backs out, X fires, Y reloads, LB uses something, RB the
  journal, View the map and Start the menu.

Sound effects and music are synthesized and played with afplay (macOS),
paplay or aplay if one is available, on Android with pyjnius, or with
pygame's mixer; otherwise the game is silent.  Music is cached in
~/.cache/lazarus.

App bundle (macOS):  lazarus --build-app [DIR]   builds lazarus.app
                     lazarus --icon-png SIZE [OUT.png]

Keys
  Arrows/WASD move, Space waits, E searches, F fires, R reloads, U uses
  something, I shows your crew member, J the journal, M the map, ? help.

The tiles are drawn by the code below (PERSON_SHAPES, ART and
draw_terrain).  --selftest builds every map, room, level and tune of a few
stations without opening a window.
"""

